WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.074 --> 00:00:08.412 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Welcome to episode two of our U.S. states series. First, I clear the map so we
can study a fresh group.

2
00:00:09.069 --> 00:00:21.654 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Our eight-state route follows the South Atlantic group: Delaware and Maryland,
Virginia and West Virginia, the Carolinas, Georgia and Florida. West Virginia
is the inland mountain member.

3
00:00:22.315 --> 00:00:29.223 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Begin with a close view of Delaware, the small state between Delaware Bay and
Maryland.

4
00:00:31.710 --> 00:00:40.626 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Delaware is this small strip below Pennsylvania, across Delaware Bay from New
Jersey. Maryland wraps around its western and southern sides.

5
00:00:41.277 --> 00:00:53.816 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Its First State nickname comes from ratification: Delaware was the first state
to ratify the United States Constitution, on December 7th, 1787.

6
00:00:54.475 --> 00:01:05.179 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Keep Delaware Bay and Chesapeake Bay separate. Delaware lies east of the
Chesapeake, on the peninsula it shares with Maryland and Virginia.

7
00:01:05.874 --> 00:01:14.059 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Now we'll widen the view toward Maryland, so its long western reach and the
Chesapeake Bay are both visible.

8
00:01:16.354 --> 00:01:27.569 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Maryland is the irregular state around the upper Chesapeake Bay. Notice its
narrow western reach and its land on both sides of the bay.

9
00:01:28.228 --> 00:01:39.490 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Its name honours Queen Henrietta Maria. The card also recalls Fort McHenry:
the flag seen there in 1814 inspired the words of The Star-Spangled Banner.

10
00:01:40.154 --> 00:01:49.326 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Use the Chesapeake as your landmark for Maryland. Virginia lies farther south,
and we will move the map to see it properly.

11
00:01:50.002 --> 00:01:59.720 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
One more move south brings Virginia fully into the study area. Watch its long
western taper and the Atlantic coast on the right.

12
00:02:01.990 --> 00:02:11.673 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Virginia lies south of Maryland and north of North Carolina. West Virginia is
a separate state on its inland side.

13
00:02:12.326 --> 00:02:25.457 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
The name honours Elizabeth the First. Its other card fact explains a nickname:
eight U.S. presidents were born in Virginia, more than in any other state.

14
00:02:26.114 --> 00:02:34.647 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Keep Virginia below Maryland and along the Atlantic. Next, we turn inland to
West Virginia and compare their shapes.

15
00:02:35.334 --> 00:02:43.217 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's compare the two Virginias. West Virginia's mountain outline lies inland
from the larger coastal state.

16
00:02:45.522 --> 00:02:56.354 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
West Virginia is the irregular mountain state west of Virginia. It is
landlocked, with Kentucky to the southwest and Ohio across the river to the
northwest.

17
00:02:57.006 --> 00:03:06.433 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
The card explains its unusual name and history: it separated from Virginia
during the Civil War and became a state in 1863.

18
00:03:07.088 --> 00:03:21.124 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember West Virginia's northern and eastern panhandles. Virginia reaches the
Atlantic; West Virginia is landlocked. Next, we return to the coast in North
Carolina.

19
00:03:21.814 --> 00:03:29.569 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Now move east to the Atlantic side. We'll follow North Carolina, South
Carolina and Georgia down the coast.

20
00:03:32.253 --> 00:03:43.294 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
North Carolina is the long state below Virginia, reaching from the mountains
to the Atlantic. Notice the thin Outer Banks along its eastern coast.

21
00:03:43.951 --> 00:03:56.060 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Carolina comes from Carolus, the Latin form of Charles. Near Kitty Hawk, the
Wright brothers made their first successful airplane flights in 1903.

22
00:03:56.721 --> 00:04:05.290 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
For your map memory, North Carolina is the broad northern Carolina, with
mountains at one end and barrier islands at the other.

23
00:04:07.600 --> 00:04:16.644 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
South Carolina sits below North Carolina and northeast of Georgia. Its compact
shape slopes southeast toward the Atlantic.

24
00:04:17.304 --> 00:04:26.987 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Its card takes us to Charleston Harbor. Confederate forces fired on Fort
Sumter there in April 1861, beginning the Civil War.

25
00:04:27.646 --> 00:04:38.095 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the Carolina pair: the longer North Carolina above, the smaller South
Carolina below. Georgia is next to the southwest.

26
00:04:40.679 --> 00:04:48.945 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Georgia lies south of Tennessee and west of South Carolina. It reaches the
Atlantic, while its wide southern border rests above Florida.

27
00:04:49.602 --> 00:05:00.179 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Its name honours King George the Second. The card also connects Georgia with
Martin Luther King, Junior, who was born in Atlanta in 1929.

28
00:05:00.832 --> 00:05:10.596 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Think of Georgia as the broad state above Florida. Alabama is to its west; now
follow the coast down onto Florida, our final state today.

29
00:05:11.290 --> 00:05:18.918 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's frame all of Florida, including its western panhandle and the peninsula
stretching south between two coasts.

30
00:05:21.538 --> 00:05:32.417 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Florida is the long peninsula below Georgia, with a panhandle extending west
toward Alabama. The Atlantic lies to its east and the Gulf to its west.

31
00:05:33.077 --> 00:05:46.208 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Its Spanish name means flowery. The Everglades in southern Florida are often
called a river of grass: a broad, shallow, slow-moving flow of water.

32
00:05:46.868 --> 00:05:57.062 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember both parts of Florida: the south-pointing peninsula and the
west-reaching panhandle. That completes our eight-state South Atlantic route.

33
00:06:02.105 --> 00:06:14.980 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Trace our eight states from Delaware and Maryland around the Chesapeake, into
Virginia and West Virginia, then down through North Carolina, South Carolina,
Georgia and Florida.

34
00:06:15.638 --> 00:06:28.223 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the two Virginias, the Carolina pair, and Florida below Georgia.
These are the eight states of the Census South Atlantic division; Washington,
D.C., is not a state.
